introduction:
  bigQuestion: "How do we organize data when we don't know how much there is?"
  overview: "This unit focuses on <strong>Dynamic Structures</strong>. Unlike Arrays (which are fixed), ADTs like <strong>Linked Lists</strong> and <strong>Trees</strong> grow and shrink as needed. You will learn how to build these structures from scratch using nodes and pointers, and explore how <strong>Hashing</strong> allows for instant data retrieval."
  journey: "You'll start by building dynamic chains (Linked Lists). Then you'll branch out into non-linear data (Binary Search Trees) and recursive traversals. Finally, you'll optimize search speed using <strong>Hash Tables</strong>."

concepts:
  - title: "Linked Lists"
    icon: "fa-diagram-project"
    content: |
      <p>A chain of nodes, connected by pointers. Easy to grow, hard to search.</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Singly Linked:</strong> Each node points to the Next one (Forward only).</li>
          <li><strong>Doubly Linked:</strong> Nodes point to Next and Previous (Bi-directional).</li>
          <li><strong>Circular:</strong> The Tail points back to the Head (No end).</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "Binary Search Trees (BST)"
    icon: "fa-tree"
    content: |
      <p>Organizing data hierarchically for faster searching.</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Rules:</strong> Left child < Parent < Right child.</li>
          <li><strong>Benefit:</strong> Cuts search space in half at every step (O(log n)).</li>
          <li><strong>Structure:</strong> Defined recursively (A tree is a node with two sub-trees).</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "Tree Traversals"
    icon: "fa-route"
    content: |
      <p>How do we visit every node in a tree?</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>In-Order:</strong> Left -> Root -> Right (Gives sorted output).</li>
          <li><strong>Pre-Order:</strong> Root -> Left -> Right (Good for copying trees).</li>
          <li><strong>Post-Order:</strong> Left -> Right -> Root (Good for deleting trees).</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "Hashing"
    icon: "fa-table"
    content: |
      <p>Finding data instantly (O(1)).</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Hash Function:</strong> Converts a Key (e.g., "Bob") into an Index (e.g., 5).</li>
          <li><strong>Collision:</strong> When two keys hash to the same index. We must handle this (e.g., by chaining).</li>
      </ul>

terminology:
  - term: "ADT (Abstract Data Type)"
    definition: "A logical description of how data is viewed and the operations that can be performed on it (Interface), separate from implementation."
  - term: "Node"
    definition: "The basic unit of a data structure, containing data and one or more pointers."
  - term: "Pointer"
    definition: "A reference to a memory location where the next node is stored."
  - term: "Root"
    definition: "The top node of a tree structure."
  - term: "Leaf"
    definition: "A node in a tree that has no children."
  - term: "Hash Function"
    definition: "An algorithm that maps data of arbitrary size to fixed-size values (indexes)."
  - term: "Collision"
    definition: "When a hash function assigns the same index to two different keys."
  - term: "Traversal"
    definition: "The process of visiting (checking/updating) each node in a tree data structure."

misconceptions:
  - mistake: "Arrays are Lists"
    correction: "Arrays are static (fixed size). Lists are dynamic (grow/shrink). Inserting into an Array is slow (shifting); inserting into a List is fast (pointer swap)."
  - mistake: "BST is always O(log n)"
    correction: "Only if balanced! A 'skewed' tree (1-2-3-4-5) is just a linked list and searches take O(n)."
  - mistake: "In-Order is default"
    correction: "Thinking In-Order is the only way. Pre-Order and Post-Order are critical for specific tasks like saving/loading structure."

